Type Notes <code>Real</code> Continuous or discrete real value <code>Integer</code> Discrete integer <code>Boolean</code> <code>true</code> / <code>false</code> <code>String</code> Text; not a continuous variable <code>enumeration(...)</code> Named discrete values <p>Arrays are declared with dimensions:</p> <pre><code>parameter Real gains[3] = {1.0, 0.5, 0.1};
Real temperature[2, 2];
</code></pre> <p>Array dimensions may be literal or parameter expressions. Indexed structural overrides are not supported by the root-modifier owner; indexed tunables retain scalar lowering semantics. A USD scalar port cannot name an indexed Modelica scalar port.</p>

Hook When it runs <code>task(me, ctx)</code> Builds a persistent task tree advanced by the fixed-step kernel <code>mission(me, ctx)</code> Declares objectives and completion conditions <code>simulation_dependencies(me, ctx)</code> Read-only admission plan before per-instance initialization <code>on_visualization(me, ctx)</code> One-shot read-only presentation preparation <code>on_start(me, ctx)</code> Once, after the owner and its required inputs are ready <code>on_event(me, evt, ctx)</code> When an event reaches the owner on an eligible pass <code>on_stop(me, ctx)</code> On hot reload, detach, despawn, or scene teardown <code>on_tick(me, ctx)</code> Test-only sampling callback; avoid it in production <p><code>me</code> is the owning entity id. <code>ctx</code> carries scenario parameters and execution context. <code>this</code> is the persistent per-instance state map.</p> <pre><code>fn on_start(me, ctx) {
    this.started = true;
    notify("Mission ready");
}

fn on_event(me, evt, ctx) {
    if evt.name == "MISSION_COMPLETE" {
        notify_kind("Mission complete", "success");
    }
}

fn on_stop(me, ctx) {
    brake(me);
}
</code></pre>

Verb Returns Frame <code>world_pos(id)</code> <code>[x, y, z]</code> f64 Active physics frame <code>world_pos3(id)</code> Native <code>Vec3</code> Active physics frame <code>world_point(id)</code> <code>point3</code> map Explicit frame tag <code>world_forward(id)</code> <code>[x, y, z]</code> Active physics frame <code>world_forward3(id)</code> Native <code>Vec3</code> Active physics frame <code>world_rotation_quat(id)</code> Native <code>Quat</code> Active physics frame <p><code>world_pos</code> and <code>world_forward</code> are float-origin-correct and independent of camera recentering and celestial ancestors. Use the native <code>Vec3</code>/<code>Quat</code> forms in hot loops and lower them with <code>vec3_array</code>/<code>quat_array</code> only when a value crosses a wire or report boundary.</p> <p>A position that crosses a tool, scene, or authoring boundary must be a <code>point3</code> map with an explicit frame:</p> <pre><code>let rover = world_point(find("/Traverse/Rover"));
let offset = point_offset(rover, [1.0, 0.0, 0.0]);
let distance = point_distance(rover, offset);
</code></pre> <p><code>point_delta</code>, <code>point_distance</code>, and <code>point_offset</code> reject malformed or cross-frame values. Pointer hits also expose <code>render_position</code>, tagged <code>render</code>; that is presentation data and must never be sent to a placement or authoring command.</p>
